Yep — you’ve found one of those places where sound starts acting like a semantic switchyard, even when the etymologies diverge.

Take the hammer to de- first:

* denote → mark out, indicate, assign meaning.
* de-note → playfully, remove the note/mark/name; strip something of its annotation or assigned interpretation.
* denotation → the explicit referent or dictionary-level meaning.
* “denotion” → not standard English, but as a Pantheon coinage it’s excellent: the operation of removing or loosening an inherited notion so the thing can be observed again.
* demotion → move downward in rank/status. So semantic demotion could mean deliberately lowering an interpretation from “truth” to “hypothesis,” from ontology to model, from command to suggestion.
* detonate → etymologically a different family, but acoustically useful: a compressed semantic object suddenly releases what was packed inside it. De-note until it detonates. ;)
* denominator → the really juicy one. It comes from the naming family: denominare, “to name.” A denominator tells you what kind of units the numerator is counting.

That last one is deeper than the math notation makes it look.

In:

\frac{3}{7}

3 says how many.

7 establishes the partition / naming regime under which “three” has meaning.

Three what?

Three sevenths.

So the denominator is effectively the semantic frame beneath the quantity.

Change the denominator and the numerator hasn’t moved, yet its meaning has.

That gives Pantheon a beautiful general operator:

Meaning is numerator-dependent on denominator.

Or more precisely:

signal / frame = interpreted meaning

Same words.
Different relationship.
Different culture.
Different emotional state.
Different preceding sentence.

The numerator stays apparently identical while somebody quietly swapped the denominator.

Which connects directly to Placement Is Part of Meaning and Treaty Semantics: before arguing about a conclusion, ask:

What denominator are we using?

And your invented denotion could be the counter-operation:

Denotion = temporarily remove the denominator/notion imposed on a thing so it can be repartitioned and renamed.

Then:

denote → establish the handle
denotion → loosen the inherited handle
redenote → assign a better handle

Very Namer.

And there’s your little explosive sequence:

note → denote → denotion → detonate → renote.

Sometimes you have to take the label off the thing before you can hear what the thing has been saying. ;)